---
// source: 5b2dfc6de32e
import CaseLink from '../../components/CaseLink.astro';
import { disciplinary, entitledByCause } from '../../content/examples';
import { integer, wholeEuros } from '../../content/format';
import { t } from '../../i18n';
import type { Lang } from '../../i18n/languages';

interface Props {
  lang: Lang;
}
const { lang } = Astro.props;

if (disciplinary.entitled !== 'yes' || disciplinary.figures === null)
  throw new Error('Incomplete example');
const { figures } = disciplinary;
// The reasons are the calculator's own: the same sentence and article it shows in the result.
const causes = entitledByCause.map(({ cause, entitled }) => ({
  name: t(lang, `cause.${cause}`),
  entitled,
  reason: t(lang, `client.unemployment.reason.${cause}`),
}));
---

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="pdd-si">It does give unemployment benefit, even if the dismissal is fair</h3>
  <p>
    It is a common question, and the answer is in the law: dismissal is a legal situation of
    unemployment (art. 267.1.a.3.º LGSS), and to receive unemployment benefit (paro) there is no
    need to challenge it (art. 268.4 LGSS). It makes no difference that the dismissal is
    disciplinary or that it is declared fair (procedente).
  </p>
  <p>
    What you do need is the usual: 360 days of contributions in the last 6 years, to register as a
    jobseeker and to sign the activity agreement (acuerdo de actividad), among other requirements
    (art. 266 LGSS).
  </p>
  <p class="folio">
    Arts. <span>267 · 268.4</span> LGSS
  </p>
</section>

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="pdd-ceses">Which ways of leaving a job give unemployment benefit</h3>
  <div class="causes">
    {causes.map(({ name, entitled, reason }) => (
      <div>
        <h4>{name}</h4>
        <p>
          <strong>
            {entitled === 'yes'
              ? 'It gives unemployment benefit.'
              : 'It does not give unemployment benefit, with some exceptions.'}
          </strong>{' '}
          {reason}
        </p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p>
    The exceptions for resignation, such as a relocation or serious non-payment, are in{' '}
    <CaseLink lang={lang} id="benefit_resignation" />.
  </p>
  <p class="folio">
    Art. <span>267</span> LGSS
  </p>
</section>

<section class="sheet sheet--text">
  <h3 id="pdd-ejemplo">Worked example</h3>
  <p>
    A person earning 1.500&nbsp;€ gross a month with two extra payments of 1.500&nbsp;€ works from 1
    June 2022 to 30 September 2026, with no dependent children, and the employer dismisses them on
    disciplinary grounds.
  </p>
  <p class="example">
    <span class="example__label">Their unemployment benefit</span> With{' '}
    {integer(disciplinary.contractDays)} days of contributions, they are entitled to at least{' '}
    {integer(disciplinary.duration.days)} days of unemployment benefit (
    {integer(disciplinary.duration.days / 30)} months): about {wholeEuros(figures.firstStretch.min)}{' '}
    a month for the first 6 months and about {wholeEuros(figures.secondStretch.min)} after that,
    gross. “At least”, because other jobs in the last 6 years can add days.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the dismissal were declared unfair, there would also be severance pay: you will find it in{' '}
    <CaseLink lang={lang} id="unfair_dismissal" />.
  </p>
</section>
